findings.description:
Untrusted user input from $_POST["target"] (line 96) flows through the commandi() function (lines 16–35) which, at the default security_level "0", applies no_check() — no sanitization at all. The returned value is concatenated directly into a shell_exec() call on lines 106 and 113:

  - Windows: shell_exec("ping -n 1 " . commandi($target));
  - Unix:    shell_exec("ping -c 1 " . commandi($target));

The output of shell_exec() is discarded (never echoed), making this a *blind* command injection. An attacker can inject arbitrary OS commands using shell metacharacters (`;`, `&&`, `||`, `|`, backticks, `$()`).

Even at security levels 1 and 2, protection depends on commandi_check_1/commandi_check_2 defined externally (functions_external.php), which may be incomplete or bypassable.

findings.recommendation:
Use parameterized queries with PDO or mysqli prepared statements instead of string concatenation. Replace the `sqli()`/`no_check()` pattern with:

```php
$stmt = $pdo->prepare("SELECT * FROM movies WHERE title = :title");
$stmt->execute(['title' => $title]);
```

Do not rely on a client-controlled cookie (`security_level`) to select sanitization — the security level must be server-side state. Remove the deprecated `mysql_*` API entirely.

Use parameterized/prepared statements instead of string concatenation to build SQL queries. Replace the deprecated `mysql_*` API with PDO or MySQLi prepared statements. For example:
```php
$stmt = $pdo->prepare("SELECT * FROM users WHERE login = ?");
$stmt->execute([$login]);
``

Use parameterized queries (prepared statements) regardless of security level. Replace the string concatenation on line 97 with:

```php
$stmt = $link->prepare("INSERT INTO visitors (date, user_agent, ip_address) VALUES (now(), ?, ?)");
$stmt->bind_param("ss", $user_agent, $ip_address);
$stmt->execute();
```

This should be the only code path — remove the reliance on a cookie-controlled security level for query safety.

Use parameterized queries (prepared statements) instead of string concatenation for all security levels. For example:

```php
$stmt = $mysqli->prepare("SELECT * FROM movies WHERE title = ?");
$stmt->bind_param("s", $title);
$stmt->execute();
```

Never rely on input escaping as the sole defense; parameterized queries should be the only code path regardless of a configurable "security level."

Use parameterized/prepared statements instead of string concatenation. Replace lines 38-42 with:

```php
$stmt = $link->prepare("UPDATE users SET secret = ? WHERE login = ?");
$stmt->bind_param("ss", $secret, $login);
$stmt->execute();
```

Additionally, the security-level gate should not be client-controlled via a cookie.
